  "metadata": {
    "title": "Addition and Subtraction of Decimals",
    "description": "Add and subtract decimals, including multi-term expressions and missing-value equations.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 40,
    "teachingApproach": "Place-value alignment → regrouping → equation solving → estimation, inverse checks, and mixed review.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 3: decimal addition/subtraction rules and solved examples 4-7 on printed pages 71-72; objective patterns in decimal sums, differences and missing values on printed pages 74-76."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Decimal addition and subtraction use base-ten place values. Align decimal points so ones, tenths, hundredths, and smaller units share their columns. Then add with carrying or subtract with borrowing. This lesson extends the method to multiple terms, missing-value equations, convenient regrouping, and checks by estimation or inverse operations. At every step, preserve the value of each term and the equality of each equation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "align_decimal_points"
        }
      ]
    },
    "align_decimal_points": {
      "id": "align_decimal_points",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Decimal points mark the boundary between whole units and tenths. Place numbers one under another so the points share a column. Then ones align with ones, tenths with tenths, and hundredths with hundredths. Appending right-hand zeroes exposes empty places without changing a terminating decimal.\n\nFor 3.6 + 0.47, write 3.60 + 0.47. The aligned sum is 4.07. Lining up final digits instead of decimal points can combine unlike units.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_align_decimal_points"
        }
      ]
    },
